put("These comparisons contextualize intervals; they do not select a preferred specification or constitute prospective power calculations. Wage intervals rule out very small positive responses but include economically substantial adjustment. Timely-certification intervals admit both 2/5/10-point gains and losses. The timing primary includes delays of 3 and 7 days; a narrower interval than 14 days does not repair identification. The earnings row describes only a pre-policy calculation and cannot substitute for the deferred outcome.")
put("## Contribution and proposed outline for the strongest direction")
put("The distinctive opportunity is employers' downward adjustment of offers after a wage-floor reduction. Bracero-exclusion research studies a labor-supply restriction and agricultural adjustment; the H-2B lottery literature studies randomized firm access to labor. Neither establishes the present statutory-dose comparison. Payroll-based nominal rigidity research studies actual paid wages, whereas these data describe offers and changing contract composition. These are focused comparators, not an exhaustive claim of novelty. [Clemens, Lewis and Postel](https://www.nber.org/papers/w23125); [Clemens and Lewis](https://www.nber.org/papers/w30589); [payroll wage rigidity study](https://www.nber.org/papers/w25470).")
put("Certification-delay concerns already appear in the [2012 GAO review](https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-12-706). A chapter contribution would require a credible disruption comparison, not simply documenting delays again. The current administrative panel does not supply that comparison reliably. The earnings track could connect an offer response to local covered-job earnings, but such a result would still combine rates, hours and composition.")
put("A conditional wage-setting chapter outline is:")
put("1. **Institution and measurement:** old/new schedules, applicability rules, the difference between a filer and a worksite business, and the unresolved legal-floor audit.\n2. **Offer adjustment:** reproduced county response, exact within/composition decomposition, then filer-state-occupation results and baseline weights.\n3. **Distribution and continuation:** common-occupation coverage, retention/reduction, reference-premium distributions, fixed heterogeneity family and all selection checks.\n4. **Credibility:** matched-window placebos, identity sensitivities, state deletions, within-filer state comparisons, concurrent processing/enforcement shocks, and the limits of those tests.\n5. **Earnings extension after publication:** the frozen complete-window test and diagnostic comparisons, clearly separated from offered wages.\n6. **Interpretation:** evidence supports substantial within-filer offer adjustment; stronger claims require verified applicable floors and a credible comparison addressing state-specific shocks.")
put("This outline is a development proposal, not promotion to a causal chapter candidate. The two concrete weaknesses to resolve are job-order/skill/worker-category applicability and confounding at the state-by-episode level. No confidential NASS microdata, institutional access or new production-linkage rebuild is required by this exploration.")
put("## Reproducibility and validation")
put("The isolated [runner and ownership README](../explorations/chapter_directions/README.md), [registry](../explorations/chapter_directions/registry.json), [amendment](../explorations/chapter_directions/measurement_amendment_2026_11_14.json) and frozen amendment hashes define this run. All large panels, raw-input hashes, source vintages, model CSVs, state deletions and failed fits live at `",out,"`. The initial provenance manifest was recorded after dependency reconstruction and the first estimates; it is a reproducibility record, not a claim that its timestamp establishes preregistration.")
